Enforce row level security in tests, and fix what that exposed

The database suite ran every query as the table owner, and Postgres skips
row security for an owner. No policy was ever evaluated: the suite would
have passed with all of them deleted. An independent review found four
policy defects sitting in exactly that blind spot.

The harness now creates the real anon, authenticated, and service_role
roles and switches into them, so policies are enforced and a defect fails
a test. auth.uid() reads request.jwt.claims as the real one does, rather
than a setting the harness invented. This needed no Docker.

Fixed, each reproduced by a failing test written from the attacker's side
first:

- date_of_birth was readable by any signed-in user. The column carried a
  comment promising otherwise, but row level security is row-level: an
  admitted row is readable in every column. Moved to profile_private,
  which has RLS enabled and no policy at all.
- Every SECURITY DEFINER function taking a user argument was an open
  endpoint, because Postgres grants EXECUTE to PUBLIC and a definer
  function bypasses RLS. This reached capability state, the age gate, and
  _rank_finalize, where a direct call would place a title without
  answering a comparison.
- Client roles held INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE on every table. AD-4 rested
  on RLS deny-by-default alone. The test meant to catch this passed
  because the roles did not exist, so it asserted an empty set was empty.
- link lists were enumerable, making them public in all but name.
  Retrieval moved to list_by_id, where naming the id is the gate.
- A block did not hide a watch tag from either party. The fix needs
  SECURITY DEFINER: a subquery against blocks inherits blocks_read, which
  hides a block from the person it should be blocking.

Also five integrity defects. The username one had the same shape as the
date of birth: reuse was credited to a primary key that had no connection
to profiles.username, so a retired name was claimable while a history row
sat there looking like protection. Now enforced by a trigger and tested by
having a second account attempt the claim.

Reporting and moderation are specified but not included. They are a new
subsystem rather than a correction, so they get their own branch and their
own review.

Reaction set recorded as a founder decision: six values including
disagree, stored as meanings rather than glyph names so the symbols stay a
copy decision. Merge authority rewritten per PRD 24.

60 tests, up from 39.
